## What was built (S222)
- `experiments/analytic/cross_x_amortisation/cross_x_hkm_decoupled.py` — instrumented Lucy DP with shared / per-x state decoupling
- 6 CSV outputs covering setup vs per-x cost, op-count scaling, M-batched amortisation, per-x scaling vs x_max, cluster-stitched anchor+sieve

## Key finding
HKM's "shareable" component is essentially trivial — just sieve of Eratosthenes up to √(x_max), O(√x_max log log) — because Lucy DP's `small[v]` is iteratively coupled to the prime step (intermediate Legendre-sieve values are NOT pi(v); only the final value is). Storing intermediate snapshots costs Θ(x_max/log x_max), defeating the amortisation. The per-x bottleneck Θ(x^{2/3}/log²) (Lagarias-Miller-Odlyzko 1985) cannot be amortised.

Empirical: setup/per-x ratio collapses {17%, 10%, 6.4%, 4.0%} as x grows; M-batched amortisation gain over M=1 is only 16% by M=32; setup share drops 6.83% → 0.24%.

